1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,310
Welcome to Module 3.

2
00:00:02,530 --> 00:00:06,596
In Module 2 you learned to
call a language model reliably.

3
00:00:06,816 --> 00:00:10,054
Now we make the model do
the right thing, consistently.

4
00:00:10,274 --> 00:00:11,621
That's prompt engineering.

5
00:00:11,841 --> 00:00:16,125
Not magic words, but clear writing,
structured outputs, and measurement.

6
00:00:16,345 --> 00:00:22,424
By the end of this module you'll build an
AI resume analyzer that turns a resume and

7
00:00:22,424 --> 00:00:25,246
a job posting into a skills-gap report.

8
00:00:26,546 --> 00:00:30,293
You'll be able to name the parts
of a prompt, and what each part does.

9
00:00:30,643 --> 00:00:33,123
Turn a vague request into a specific one.

10
00:00:33,473 --> 00:00:36,859
State constraints plainly,
with the reason behind them.

11
00:00:37,209 --> 00:00:41,522
And recognise prompting advice
that current models no longer need.

12
00:00:42,822 --> 00:00:45,215
A good prompt usually has five parts.

13
00:00:45,435 --> 00:00:49,810
Not every prompt needs all five, but
knowing them tells you what's missing.

14
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:51,414
First, context.

15
00:00:51,634 --> 00:00:54,587
Who is the output for,
and what happens to it next?

16
00:00:54,807 --> 00:00:58,933
This is the part beginners leave
out, and the part that matters most.

17
00:00:59,283 --> 00:01:03,050
Second, the task: one clear
sentence saying what to produce.

18
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:08,572
Third, the data: the document the model
works on, kept clearly separate from your

19
00:01:08,572 --> 00:01:09,402
instructions.

20
00:01:09,752 --> 00:01:14,047
Fourth, constraints: what counts,
what doesn't, and the shape of the answer.

21
00:01:14,397 --> 00:01:18,288
And fifth, examples, when the
format or the judgment is subtle.

22
00:01:18,508 --> 00:01:20,547
We'll cover those in the next lesson.

23
00:01:21,847 --> 00:01:26,199
Here are two prompts for the same
task, both from this module's project.

24
00:01:26,419 --> 00:01:31,673
The first one is how most people start:
a generic role, and one short instruction.

25
00:01:31,893 --> 00:01:35,281
It isn't wrong, but it
leaves every decision to the model.

26
00:01:35,501 --> 00:01:36,668
Which skills count?

27
00:01:36,888 --> 00:01:38,093
Soft skills too?

28
00:01:38,313 --> 00:01:39,384
In what format?

29
00:01:39,604 --> 00:01:42,440
Should it guess skills
the resume only implies?

30
00:01:42,790 --> 00:01:48,092
The second version says who uses the
output, and what happens to it: code compares

31
00:01:48,092 --> 00:01:49,515
it with a job posting.

32
00:01:49,735 --> 00:01:55,371
That one sentence explains why accuracy
matters more than completeness, so the model

33
00:01:55,371 --> 00:01:59,665
can make the right call in
situations the prompt never mentions.

34
00:02:00,965 --> 00:02:03,954
Context is the cheapest
improvement you can make.

35
00:02:04,174 --> 00:02:07,264
Say who the output is
for, and how it will be used.

36
00:02:07,614 --> 00:02:11,222
Describe the quality bar:
what does a great answer look like?

37
00:02:11,572 --> 00:02:13,975
And explain the reason behind each rule.

38
00:02:14,195 --> 00:02:18,602
A rule with a reason
generalises to cases you never thought of.

39
00:02:18,822 --> 00:02:20,054
A bare rule doesn't.

40
00:02:20,404 --> 00:02:25,763
When a prompt is too short, the model
fills the gaps with safe defaults, and you get

41
00:02:25,763 --> 00:02:26,720
generic output.

42
00:02:26,940 --> 00:02:29,365
Give more context than seems necessary.

43
00:02:30,665 --> 00:02:35,396
You'll find prompts full of capital
letters: critical, must, never, important.

44
00:02:35,616 --> 00:02:40,508
That style was written for older
models that sometimes ignored instructions.

45
00:02:40,728 --> 00:02:45,895
Current models follow instructions
closely, so shouting makes them over-apply the

46
00:02:45,895 --> 00:02:50,807
rule, and stacked prohibitions can
even pull them toward the very mistake you

47
00:02:50,807 --> 00:02:51,318
mention.

48
00:02:51,668 --> 00:02:56,901
Instead, state the rule once, in a
normal voice, say exactly what counts, and give

49
00:02:56,901 --> 00:02:57,603
the reason.

50
00:02:57,823 --> 00:03:03,447
A concrete edge case, like "no
experience with Kubernetes", is worth more than any

51
00:03:03,447 --> 00:03:05,230
number of capital letters.

52
00:03:06,530 --> 00:03:08,473
Prompting advice ages quickly.

53
00:03:08,694 --> 00:03:13,777
Here are four patterns you'll still see
in tutorials, and what to use instead on

54
00:03:13,777 --> 00:03:15,175
current Claude models.

55
00:03:15,525 --> 00:03:16,766
Think step by step.

56
00:03:16,986 --> 00:03:22,429
Current models already reason before
they answer, and you control how much with the

57
00:03:22,429 --> 00:03:25,380
effort setting from
Module 2, not with prose.

58
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:31,061
Reply only in JSON, followed by
regular expressions to dig the JSON out.

59
00:03:31,281 --> 00:03:36,661
Use structured outputs instead: the
API constrains the reply to your schema.

60
00:03:36,881 --> 00:03:38,072
That's lesson three.

61
00:03:38,422 --> 00:03:43,550
Prefilling, where you start the
assistant's reply for it, is rejected with a four

62
00:03:43,550 --> 00:03:46,019
hundred error on current Claude models.

63
00:03:46,239 --> 00:03:48,064
Structured outputs replace it.

64
00:03:48,414 --> 00:03:52,645
And don't add a reasoning field to
your JSON for the model to fill in.

65
00:03:52,865 --> 00:03:56,851
If you need to know why, ask for
a short explanation of the answer.

66
00:03:58,151 --> 00:03:59,891
Is the longer prompt expensive?

67
00:04:00,111 --> 00:04:03,261
This script renders both
versions for a short resume.

68
00:04:03,481 --> 00:04:06,835
Version two is about
two hundred tokens longer.

69
00:04:07,055 --> 00:04:12,202
At four dollars per million input
tokens, that's less than a tenth of a cent per

70
00:04:12,202 --> 00:04:12,524
call.

71
00:04:12,744 --> 00:04:15,997
Input tokens are cheap,
and wrong answers are not.

72
00:04:17,297 --> 00:04:18,105
Quick check.

73
00:04:18,325 --> 00:04:20,678
Which change most improves a vague prompt?

74
00:04:26,378 --> 00:04:29,864
Saying who uses the
output, and why accuracy matters.

75
00:04:30,084 --> 00:04:35,795
Context and reasons let the model make
good decisions in cases you never listed.

76
00:04:37,095 --> 00:04:42,063
A prompt has context, a task, data,
constraints, and sometimes examples.

77
00:04:42,413 --> 00:04:44,946
Context and reasons beat bare rules.

78
00:04:45,296 --> 00:04:48,113
Write in plain language, at normal volume.

79
00:04:48,463 --> 00:04:51,959
And use effort and
structured outputs, not old tricks.

80
00:04:52,309 --> 00:04:57,966
Next lesson: examples, separating data
from instructions with tags, and splitting a

81
00:04:57,966 --> 00:04:59,875
big task into smaller steps.
